select 'bbbb0003-0000-4000-8000-000000000019', v.pos, v.title, v.kind, v.body::jsonb, v.notes, v.mins
from (values
  (1, 'Diagnostic: where are you now?', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"The clearest difference between management and leadership is that management is mostly about…","options":["inspiring a shared direction","coping with complexity — planning, budgeting, and control","personal charisma","avoiding all conflict"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Management copes with complexity through planning and control; leadership copes with change by setting direction and motivating people — see ''Leadership vs management''."},{"prompt":"Emotional intelligence begins with which capability?","options":["persuading others","self-awareness — noticing your own emotions as they happen","winning arguments","hiding what you feel"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Self-awareness is the foundation; you cannot manage a reaction you have not noticed — see ''Self-awareness and emotional intelligence''."},{"prompt":"According to Herzberg, fixing a low salary or bad working conditions mainly…","options":["creates strong lasting motivation","removes dissatisfaction but does not by itself motivate","has no effect at all","always reduces performance"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Pay and conditions are hygiene factors: their absence dissatisfies, but their presence only prevents dissatisfaction — motivators like achievement drive engagement."},{"prompt":"In Tuckman''s model, the stage where a new team openly disagrees about roles and approach is called…","options":["forming","storming","norming","performing"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Storming is the friction stage; teams that name it can move on to norming and performing — see ''Building and leading effective teams''."},{"prompt":"Active listening is best described as…","options":["waiting quietly for your turn to talk","reflecting back what you heard to confirm understanding","giving advice quickly","taking detailed notes silently"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Active listening means checking understanding out loud, not just staying silent — see ''Communication and active listening''."},{"prompt":"When you delegate a task, you hand over…","options":["accountability, so it is no longer your concern","the work and the authority to do it, while staying accountable for the outcome","nothing real — you should redo it yourself","only the blame if it fails"],"answer":1,"explanation":"You transfer responsibility and authority but remain accountable overall — see ''Delegation and coaching''."},{"prompt":"The most useful feedback is…","options":["a general judgment like ''be more professional''","specific and behavioral — what happened and its effect","saved up for the annual review","always sandwiched between two compliments"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Specific, behavioral, timely feedback is actionable; vague labels are not — see ''Giving and receiving feedback''."},{"prompt":"A common reason people resist organizational change is…","options":["they always prefer novelty","fear of loss and uncertainty about what it means for them","they never read email","change is illegal"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Resistance is usually about perceived loss and uncertainty, not stubbornness — see ''Leading change and decision-making''."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (2, 'Leadership vs management and core leadership theories', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Distinguish management from leadership, and name the core theories — trait, behavioral, contingency, transactional, and transformational — well enough to say which fits a situation. Warm-up: from the diagnostic, is ''planning and budgeting'' more a management task or a leadership task?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Management copes with complexity: it plans, budgets, organizes, staffs, and controls so that a known system runs reliably. Leadership copes with change: it sets a direction, aligns people around it, and motivates them to push through obstacles. You need both, and one person usually does both. The theories map how thinking evolved. Trait theory asked what leaders are like and found no single winning personality. Behavioral theory shifted to what leaders do, describing task-oriented behavior (structure, goals) and people-oriented behavior (support, relationships). Contingency theory added that it depends on the situation: the right style varies with the task, the followers, and the pressure. Transactional leadership works through exchange — clear goals, rewards for hitting them, correction when they are missed. Transformational leadership works through inspiration — a compelling vision, individual attention, and raising people''s sights beyond their own immediate interest. Strong leaders blend the two: transactional systems keep the trains running, transformational energy gives people a reason to care."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"A shift supervisor who writes the rota, checks stock, and enforces safety rules is doing management and transactional leadership. A team lead who paints a picture of why the new product matters and coaches each engineer individually is doing transformational leadership. Contingency thinking in action: with a brand-new, unsure team you lean task-oriented and give clear structure; with a seasoned, capable team you step back and lean people-oriented, offering support rather than instructions. Behavioral lens: praising effort and asking about someone''s weekend is people-oriented behavior; setting a deadline and clarifying who owns what is task-oriented behavior. A good manager uses both dials, not just one."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Classify ''preparing next quarter''s budget'': management or leadership? 2) A leader offers a bonus for hitting a sales target — transactional or transformational? 3) A leader spends an hour helping someone see how their work connects to the mission — which style? 4) Trait theory failed to find what? 5) Contingency theory''s one-sentence claim is? 6) Name the two broad behavior categories from behavioral theory. Answers: 1) Management. 2) Transactional (reward for an outcome). 3) Transformational (vision and individual attention). 4) A single set of traits that reliably identifies leaders. 5) The best leadership style depends on the situation. 6) Task-oriented and people-oriented behavior."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: treating ''manager'' as a demotion from ''leader'' — they are different work, both essential. Mistake: assuming transformational is always better; without transactional structure, inspiration has nothing to run on. Exit: (a) Management copes mainly with what, and leadership with what? (b) What does contingency theory add to behavioral theory? Answers: management copes with complexity, leadership copes with change; contingency adds that the effective style depends on the situation."}]}'::jsonb, 'Managers and leaders are roles you switch between, not personality types — the same person plans a budget in the morning and rallies the team in the afternoon.', 14),
  (3, 'Self-awareness and emotional intelligence', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Break emotional intelligence into its parts, use a simple trigger-pause-response habit, and seek feedback to shrink your blind spots. Warm-up: which capability does emotional intelligence start with, according to the diagnostic?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Emotional intelligence is the ability to notice and manage emotions — your own and other people''s — in the service of good decisions. It has four parts. Self-awareness is noticing what you feel and how it colors your judgment. Self-management is choosing your response instead of firing off a reaction. Social awareness, including empathy, is reading the room and understanding what others are feeling. Relationship management is using all of that to communicate, influence, and handle conflict well. The engine underneath is a simple loop: a trigger arrives, there is a gap, and then a response. Under stress the gap shrinks to zero and you react automatically. The skill is widening that gap — pause, name the feeling, and pick a response on purpose. Blind spots are the catch: everyone has behaviors others see clearly and they do not. The only reliable fix is to ask for feedback and treat it as data."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"A teammate publicly picks apart your plan. Low emotional intelligence: you feel the heat, snap back, and the meeting derails. Higher emotional intelligence: you notice the flush of defensiveness (self-awareness), take a breath (self-management), ask ''what specifically worries you?'' (social awareness), and turn an attack into a working conversation (relationship management). Another case: you keep interrupting in meetings and do not realize it. A trusted colleague mentions it. Rather than defending, you say ''thank you, I''ll watch for that'' — and ask them to signal you if it happens again. That is closing a blind spot with feedback. Self-management example: you get a terse email that reads as rude; instead of replying immediately, you wait an hour, and the calmer reply prevents a spiral."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Which part of emotional intelligence is ''reading how the room is feeling''? 2) In the trigger-gap-response loop, stress does what to the gap? 3) What is the most reliable way to discover a blind spot? 4) Name the four parts of emotional intelligence. 5) True or false: naming an emotion makes it stronger. 6) A colleague gives you hard feedback; the emotionally intelligent first move is? Answers: 1) Social awareness (empathy). 2) It shrinks the gap toward zero, making you react automatically. 3) Ask others for candid feedback and take it as information. 4) Self-awareness, self-management, social awareness, relationship management. 5) False — labeling a feeling tends to reduce its intensity. 6) Listen and thank them before explaining or defending."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: confusing self-management with suppression — the goal is to choose a response, not to bottle everything up. Mistake: treating empathy as agreement; you can understand someone''s feeling without endorsing their position. Exit: (a) What is the difference between self-awareness and self-management? (b) Why does feedback matter for blind spots? Answers: self-awareness is noticing the feeling, self-management is choosing what to do with it; feedback is the only way to see behavior you cannot observe in yourself."}]}'::jsonb, 'Naming an emotion (''I''m frustrated'') is not weakness — labeling a feeling reliably lowers its grip and buys you a moment to choose your response.', 13),
  (4, 'Motivation: intrinsic and extrinsic, Maslow, Herzberg, and autonomy-mastery-purpose', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Tell intrinsic from extrinsic motivation, apply Maslow''s hierarchy and Herzberg''s two-factor theory, and use the autonomy-mastery-purpose frame to design more motivating work. Warm-up: from the diagnostic, does fixing a low salary create lasting motivation or just remove dissatisfaction?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Extrinsic motivation comes from outside — pay, bonuses, praise, avoiding punishment. Intrinsic motivation comes from the work itself — interest, challenge, meaning. Both matter, but they behave differently. Maslow arranged needs in a rough hierarchy: physiological, safety, belonging, esteem, and self-actualization; lower needs generally pull hardest until they are reasonably met. Herzberg split the picture in two. Hygiene factors — pay, conditions, policies, job security — cause dissatisfaction when they are bad, but fixing them only returns you to neutral. Motivators — achievement, recognition, responsibility, growth — are what actually create engagement. So paying market rate removes a complaint; giving someone a meaningful challenge is what lights them up. The modern shorthand, drawn from self-determination research, is autonomy, mastery, and purpose: people are most engaged when they have some control over how they work, a sense of getting better, and a reason that matters. As a manager you cannot inject intrinsic motivation, but you can design conditions where it grows."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"A developer is underpaid and frustrated. You raise pay to market rate: the frustration goes, but she is not suddenly inspired — that was a hygiene fix. To motivate her, you hand her a hard, visible problem she can own (achievement, autonomy). Purpose in action: a support team''s numbers climb after the lead starts each week by reading one real customer story showing the impact of their help. Autonomy in action: letting engineers choose which of two approaches to try, within a deadline, raises ownership versus dictating the method. Mastery in action: a weekly thirty-minute learning slot signals that getting better is part of the job. Crowding-out caution: a team that volunteered enthusiastically for a side project lost interest once a small per-task bonus reframed it as paid work — the reward replaced the fun."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) A gym membership perk — intrinsic or extrinsic? 2) On Herzberg''s map, is ''a fair salary'' a motivator or a hygiene factor? 3) Which Maslow level covers ''feeling part of the team''? 4) Name the three elements of the autonomy-mastery-purpose frame. 5) Give one risk of paying people to do something they already enjoy. 6) True or false: fixing hygiene factors produces strong motivation. Answers: 1) Extrinsic. 2) Hygiene factor. 3) Belonging. 4) Autonomy, mastery, purpose. 5) It can crowd out the intrinsic motivation, reducing effort once the reward stops. 6) False — it removes dissatisfaction but does not by itself motivate."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: reaching for money first for every motivation problem, when the real gap is meaning, growth, or control. Mistake: assuming everyone is motivated by the same thing — ask people what matters to them. Exit: (a) What is the key difference between hygiene factors and motivators? (b) What are the three parts of autonomy-mastery-purpose? Answers: hygiene factors prevent dissatisfaction while motivators create engagement; autonomy, mastery, and purpose."}]}'::jsonb, 'Rewards are not free — pay people for something they already enjoy and you can crowd out the intrinsic motivation that was driving them. Use extrinsic rewards deliberately.', 14),
  (5, 'Building and leading effective teams: Tuckman and psychological safety', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Recognize Tuckman''s five stages, understand why psychological safety predicts team performance, and take concrete steps to build it. Warm-up: from the diagnostic, which Tuckman stage is marked by open disagreement about roles and approach?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Tuckman described how groups develop through five stages: forming (polite, unsure, dependent on the leader), storming (friction over roles, goals, and methods), norming (agreement on how to work together settles in), performing (the team delivers with little friction), and adjourning (the team wraps up and disbands). Progress is not automatic; teams can get stuck in storming, and adding a new member can bounce a group back a stage. The deeper factor underneath high performance is psychological safety: the shared belief that you can speak up, ask a question, admit a mistake, or disagree without being punished or humiliated. In safe teams people report problems early, ideas flow, and learning is fast. In unsafe teams people go quiet, hide errors, and defer to the loudest voice. As a leader you build safety by inviting input directly, responding to bad news with curiosity rather than blame, admitting your own mistakes, and rewarding the messenger. A capable team without safety underperforms a modest team that has it."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"A newly formed project team is all politeness for a week (forming), then erupts into disagreement about scope and who decides (storming). Instead of panicking, the lead names it: ''This tension is normal; let''s agree how we''ll make decisions.'' They set working agreements (norming) and soon ship steadily (performing). Psychological safety in action: an engineer says in standup, ''I think I introduced the bug in yesterday''s release.'' The lead replies, ''Thanks for flagging it fast — let''s fix it and see what made it easy to miss,'' rather than assigning blame. Contrast: on an unsafe team the same engineer stays silent, the bug reaches customers, and the lesson is ''never admit anything.'' Building safety: a manager who ends each retro by asking ''what did we learn?'' and shares her own miss first makes it safe for others to do the same."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Which Tuckman stage comes right after forming? 2) A team agrees on norms and starts cooperating smoothly — which stage? 3) Define psychological safety in one sentence. 4) Name one leader behavior that builds safety. 5) True or false: adding a new member can push a team back to an earlier stage. 6) What tends to happen to error reporting on low-safety teams? Answers: 1) Storming. 2) Norming. 3) The shared belief that you can speak up, question, or admit mistakes without punishment or humiliation. 4) Responding to bad news with curiosity, admitting your own mistakes, or thanking people for flagging problems. 5) True. 6) People hide errors, so problems surface late."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: reading storming as failure and suppressing all conflict, which just delays the real conversation. Mistake: confusing psychological safety with lowered standards — safety is about candor, not comfort or leniency. Exit: (a) List Tuckman''s five stages in order. (b) Why does psychological safety improve performance? Answers: forming, storming, norming, performing, adjourning; because people surface problems and ideas early instead of hiding them, so the team learns and adjusts faster."}]}'::jsonb, '''Storming'' is not a sign your team is broken — it is a normal, often necessary stage. Naming it out loud usually shortens it.', 14),
  (6, 'Checkpoint 1', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Leadership, as opposed to management, is primarily about…","options":["planning and control of a known system","coping with change by setting direction and motivating people","enforcing policy","budgeting"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Management copes with complexity; leadership copes with change."},{"prompt":"Offering a clear reward for hitting a defined target is an example of which style?","options":["transformational","transactional","trait-based","laissez-faire"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Transactional leadership works through exchange — rewards for meeting goals."},{"prompt":"In the trigger-gap-response loop, stress tends to…","options":["widen the gap so you respond thoughtfully","shrink the gap so you react automatically","have no effect","remove the trigger"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Under stress the gap collapses, producing automatic reactions instead of chosen responses."},{"prompt":"On Herzberg''s two-factor theory, recognition and achievement are…","options":["hygiene factors","motivators","physiological needs","irrelevant"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Recognition, achievement, responsibility, and growth are motivators that drive engagement."},{"prompt":"The Tuckman stage marked by friction over roles and methods is…","options":["forming","storming","norming","adjourning"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Storming is the conflict stage that often precedes real cooperation."},{"prompt":"Psychological safety most directly increases a team''s…","options":["willingness to speak up and admit mistakes","office perks","headcount","meeting length"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Safety means people can raise problems and ideas without fear, so issues surface early."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (7, 'Communication and active listening', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Run the communication loop deliberately, practice active listening, and frame messages with ''I'' statements. Warm-up: from the diagnostic, what does active listening actually require you to do?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Communication is a loop, not a broadcast: a sender encodes a message, it travels through a channel, a receiver decodes it, and feedback confirms whether meaning arrived. Noise — distraction, jargon, assumptions, emotion — distorts it at every step, which is why ''I said it'' is not the same as ''they understood it.'' Active listening closes the loop on purpose. You give full attention, hold your reply, and reflect back what you heard: ''So the deadline is the real worry — have I got that right?'' This does three things: it checks accuracy, it signals respect, and it slows a heated exchange enough to think. Open questions (''what would make this easier?'') pull out more than closed ones. Framing matters too: ''I'' statements report your experience and keep the other person open, while ''you'' statements sound like accusations and trigger defense. Most workplace friction is not disagreement about facts; it is meaning that never fully crossed the gap."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"A report is late again. ''You'' framing: ''You never send updates on time'' — the person defends and nothing changes. ''I'' framing: ''When the update arrives late, I get blocked and end up rushing — can we find a time that works?'' Now it is a problem to solve together. Active listening in action: a frustrated teammate vents for two minutes; instead of jumping to solutions you say, ''It sounds like the shifting requirements are what''s exhausting — is that the main thing?'' They feel heard and become open to fixing it. Reducing noise: before a big ask, you replace jargon with plain words and finish with ''what questions do you have?'' rather than ''does that make sense?'', which invites a real response instead of a reflexive nod."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) In the communication loop, what confirms the message was understood? 2) Rewrite ''you''re always interrupting me'' as an ''I'' statement. 3) Is ''what would make this easier for you?'' an open or closed question? 4) Name one common source of noise. 5) What are the two jobs active listening does beyond gathering information? 6) True or false: saying it clearly guarantees it was understood. Answers: 1) Feedback from the receiver. 2) Sample: ''I lose my train of thought when I''m interrupted — can I finish, then hear you?'' 3) Open. 4) Distraction, jargon, assumptions, or strong emotion (any one). 5) It signals respect and slows a heated exchange. 6) False — understanding depends on decoding, not just sending."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: mistaking silence for listening — if you never reflect back, the speaker cannot tell you understood. Mistake: rushing to solutions before the person feels heard, which makes good advice bounce off. Exit: (a) What does reflecting back accomplish? (b) Why do ''I'' statements de-escalate better than ''you'' statements? Answers: it checks that meaning arrived and shows respect; ''I'' statements report your experience without accusing, so the other person stays open instead of defending."}]}'::jsonb, '''I'' statements describe your experience (''I felt blocked when the update was late'') and invite dialogue; ''you'' statements (''you never update me'') invite defense.', 13),
  (8, 'Delegation and coaching', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Delegate at the right level, avoid the two failure modes of dumping and micromanaging, and use a simple coaching model to grow people. Warm-up: from the diagnostic, when you delegate, what stays with you even after you hand over the work?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Delegation transfers the work and the authority to do it, while you remain accountable for the result. Done well it multiplies your capacity and develops people; done badly it fails in one of two directions. Dumping is handing over a task with no context, no clarity on ''done,'' and no support — then acting surprised when it goes wrong. Micromanaging is handing it over and then hovering, correcting every keystroke, which teaches people to stop thinking. The middle path is to match your involvement to the person''s skill and confidence on that specific task: high structure and check-ins for something new to them, a light touch for something they have mastered. Coaching is the longer game — helping someone find their own answer instead of handing them yours. A useful frame is GROW: Goal (what do you want?), Reality (what''s actually happening now?), Options (what could you try?), and Will (what will you do, and by when?). Coaching questions build capability; giving answers builds dependence."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Delegating well: ''I''d like you to own the release notes. Done means every user-facing change is listed in plain language, published by Thursday. Here''s last month''s as a model — come to me if you hit anything unclear.'' Clear outcome, deadline, authority, and a safety net, without dictating wording. Dumping, by contrast: ''Handle the release notes'' with no example, no deadline, no offer of help. Micromanaging: assigning it, then rewriting every line yourself. Coaching with GROW: a report is stuck on a client issue. Instead of solving it, you ask, ''What outcome do you want here?'' (Goal), ''What have you tried and what happened?'' (Reality), ''What options do you see?'' (Options), ''Which will you do, and when?'' (Will). They leave with a plan they own — and the skill to handle the next one."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) When you delegate, who stays accountable for the outcome? 2) Name the two failure modes of delegation. 3) In GROW, what does the R stand for? 4) For a task brand-new to someone, do you use more or less structure? 5) Why prefer a coaching question over just giving the answer? 6) One sentence: what should ''done'' include when you delegate? Answers: 1) You do — accountability stays with the delegator. 2) Dumping and micromanaging. 3) Reality (what''s happening now). 4) More structure and more frequent check-ins. 5) It builds the person''s capability instead of your dependence. 6) A clear definition of the outcome and a deadline (and the authority to act)."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: delegating the task but withholding the authority, so the person has to come back for every decision. Mistake: switching to coaching questions in a genuine emergency — when the building is on fire, give the answer. Exit: (a) What three things do you transfer or set when delegating well? (b) What are the four steps of GROW? Answers: the work, the authority, and a clear outcome with a deadline; Goal, Reality, Options, Will."}]}'::jsonb, 'Delegate outcomes, not keystrokes — say what ''done'' looks like and by when, then let the person choose the how. Dictating the method is just doing the work through someone else.', 13),
  (9, 'Giving and receiving feedback', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Give specific, behavioral feedback with a clear structure, receive feedback without getting defensive, and separate praise from problem-solving. Warm-up: from the diagnostic, what makes feedback useful rather than useless?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Good feedback is specific, behavioral, and timely. Specific means a concrete instance, not a trend. Behavioral means what a camera would have seen, not a judgment of character. Timely means close to the event, not saved for a review months later. A reliable structure is situation-behavior-impact: name the situation, describe the observable behavior, then state its impact. This keeps you out of mind-reading and gives the person something they can actually change. Feedback also needs a two-way door: end by asking for their view, because you might be missing context. Receiving feedback is a skill of its own. The instinct is to defend; the discipline is to get curious. Assume there is at least a grain of truth, ask a clarifying question, thank the person, and decide what to do later when you are calm. Separately, do not blend praise and criticism into a mushy ''sandwich'' — people learn to brace for the bad news and discount the praise. Give genuine recognition on its own, and deliver problems clearly on their own."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Situation-behavior-impact in action: ''In this morning''s client call (situation), you answered the pricing question before the client finished asking (behavior), and they went quiet and didn''t raise their other concerns (impact). What did you notice?'' Specific, observable, and it opens a conversation. Weak version: ''You were kind of rude on the call'' — vague, characterizing, and easy to reject. Receiving well: a peer says your slides were too dense. Instead of ''they had all the required detail,'' you say, ''Thanks — which slide lost you?'' and learn something. Praise done right: ''The way you calmed that upset customer today was excellent — you let them finish and then owned the fix,'' given on its own, not stapled to a criticism. Timeliness: flagging a missed handoff the same afternoon lands far better than raising it in a quarterly review."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Name the three qualities of useful feedback. 2) What do the letters in situation-behavior-impact stand for? 3) Rewrite ''you''re disorganized'' as behavioral feedback. 4) What is the first move when receiving hard feedback? 5) Why avoid the praise-criticism-praise sandwich? 6) True or false: feedback is best saved for the annual review. Answers: 1) Specific, behavioral, timely. 2) Situation, behavior, impact. 3) Sample: ''The last two reports arrived after the deadline, so the team had to rush the review.'' 4) Get curious — ask a clarifying question rather than defend. 5) People learn to brace for the criticism and discount the praise. 6) False — feedback works best close to the event."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: labeling the person (''lazy,'' ''careless'') instead of describing behavior, which guarantees defensiveness. Mistake: only ever giving corrective feedback, so recognition never registers and morale erodes. Exit: (a) What are the three parts of situation-behavior-impact? (b) When receiving feedback, what should you do before deciding whether you agree? Answers: situation, behavior, impact; listen and get curious — ask for specifics and thank them, then judge it later when calm."}]}'::jsonb, 'Feedback describes behavior and its effect, not character. ''The report went out with three errors'' is workable; ''you''re careless'' is a verdict that invites defense.', 13),
  (10, 'Checkpoint 2', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Reflecting back what you heard — ''so the deadline is the real worry?'' — is a core move in…","options":["passive listening","active listening","broadcasting","note-taking"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Active listening checks understanding out loud and signals respect."},{"prompt":"An ''I'' statement works better than a ''you'' statement mainly because it…","options":["is shorter","reports your experience without accusing, so the other person stays open","hides your real view","is more formal"],"answer":1,"explanation":"''I'' statements describe impact on you and invite dialogue instead of triggering defense."},{"prompt":"When you delegate a task, who remains accountable for the outcome?","options":["the person you delegated to, entirely","you, the delegator","no one","the whole team equally"],"answer":1,"explanation":"You transfer the work and authority but stay accountable for the result."},{"prompt":"In the GROW coaching model, the ''O'' stands for…","options":["Objectives","Options","Outcomes","Obstacles"],"answer":1,"explanation":"GROW is Goal, Reality, Options, Will."},{"prompt":"Which piece of feedback is specific and behavioral rather than a character judgment?","options":["You''re careless","You need a better attitude","The report went out with three errors this morning","You''re just not a detail person"],"answer":2,"explanation":"It names an observable behavior and instance the person can act on."},{"prompt":"A drawback of the praise-criticism-praise ''sandwich'' is that people tend to…","options":["remember only the praise","brace for the criticism and discount the praise","ignore all of it equally","feel over-praised"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The pattern teaches people to wait for the bad news, diluting genuine recognition."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (11, 'Managing conflict and difficult conversations', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Read conflict-handling styles, choose one on purpose, and open a difficult conversation without triggering a fight. Warm-up: from the diagnostic, a difference over how to do the work is usually which kind of conflict — task or relationship?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Not all conflict is bad. Task conflict — disagreement about ideas and approaches — can sharpen decisions when handled well. Relationship conflict — personal friction and animosity — almost always hurts. The manager''s job is to keep disagreement on the task and off the person. The Thomas-Kilmann framework maps five styles along two axes, assertiveness and cooperativeness: competing (I win), accommodating (you win), avoiding (nobody engages), compromising (split the difference), and collaborating (find a solution that meets both sides'' real interests). No style is always right — you compete on a safety non-negotiable, accommodate on something you barely care about, collaborate when the relationship and the outcome both matter. The unlock in most hard cases is to move from positions to interests: behind ''I want Fridays off'' might be ''I need predictable time for childcare,'' which opens options a position never would. For the conversation itself: prepare, open with the facts and your intent, ask for their view and truly listen, then problem-solve toward a shared outcome. Address issues early, privately, and directly — avoidance lets small frictions harden."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Two engineers clash over the architecture. You keep it task conflict: ''We both want this to scale — let''s compare the two designs against that, not defend our egos.'' Positions to interests: one insists on a bigger budget (position); the interest underneath is ''I don''t want to be blamed when we run out mid-quarter.'' Naming that opens a solution — a spending checkpoint — that the budget fight never could. Choosing a style: a teammate wants the good conference room for a client demo and you wanted it for a routine sync — you accommodate, because you barely care and it buys goodwill. Opening a hard conversation: ''I want to talk about yesterday''s meeting because I value working well with you (intent). When the numbers were challenged in front of the client (facts), I felt caught off guard. How did you see it?'' Facts plus intent plus a real question, not an ambush."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Which conflict type tends to help decisions when handled well? 2) In Thomas-Kilmann, which style is high assertiveness and high cooperativeness? 3) Give the two axes the five styles are mapped on. 4) Translate the position ''I want a raise'' into a possible underlying interest. 5) When is ''avoiding'' a defensible choice? 6) Name two things to include when opening a difficult conversation. Answers: 1) Task conflict. 2) Collaborating. 3) Assertiveness and cooperativeness. 4) Sample: recognition, fairness versus peers, or financial security. 5) When the issue is genuinely trivial or emotions are too hot to talk productively right now. 6) The facts and your intent (and a genuine request for their view)."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: defaulting to avoiding for everything, which lets small issues fester into relationship conflict. Mistake: arguing positions back and forth without ever asking why each side wants what they want. Exit: (a) What is the difference between task and relationship conflict? (b) Why move from positions to interests? Answers: task conflict is about ideas and can be productive, relationship conflict is personal and usually harmful; interests reveal the real needs, which often overlap and open solutions that clashing positions hide."}]}'::jsonb, 'Separate positions (what each side demands) from interests (why they want it). Positions clash; interests often overlap, and that overlap is where agreement lives.', 14),
  (12, 'Leading change and decision-making', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Lead a change using a clear sequence, anticipate why people resist, and pick a decision-making approach that fits the situation. Warm-up: from the diagnostic, what is usually driving resistance to change?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Change fails more often from neglected people than from bad plans. A useful sequence, drawn from Kotter''s work, runs: establish urgency (why now, and what happens if we don''t), build a guiding coalition (a credible group backing it), craft and communicate a clear vision (over-communicate it), empower action by removing obstacles, generate short-term wins so people see progress, consolidate gains without declaring victory too early, and anchor the change in how things are normally done. Lewin''s simpler frame is unfreeze (loosen the current way), change (move to the new way), refreeze (make it the new normal). Resistance is predictable and mostly rational: people fear losing competence, status, security, or control, or they simply do not understand the why. You address it by involving people early, being honest about trade-offs, and naming what will and will not change. On decisions, match the mode to the moment: decide alone when speed matters and you have the information; consult when others hold knowledge you lack; build consensus when buy-in is essential and time allows. Beware of biases — confirmation bias, anchoring, groupshink, sunk-cost thinking — and slow down for the decisions that are hard to reverse."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Rolling out a new process: instead of announcing it Monday, you first explain why the old way is failing (urgency), get respected team members on board (coalition), paint the specific ''after'' picture (vision), clear a blocking approval (empower), and celebrate the first team to adopt it (short-term win). Resistance, decoded: a senior engineer stonewalls a new tool not from stubbornness but because he fears looking slow while he relearns — pairing him with training and time dissolves it. Decision modes: a production outage is a decide-alone moment; choosing a new vendor is a consult moment; changing the team''s core working agreements is a build-consensus moment because everyone has to live it. Reversibility check: you move fast on the reversible choice of trying a new standup format for two weeks, and slowly on the hard-to-undo choice of a reorganization."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) In Lewin''s model, what are the three steps? 2) Name the first step in Kotter''s sequence. 3) Give two things people commonly fear losing in a change. 4) Which decision mode fits ''others hold knowledge you lack''? 5) Why generate short-term wins during a change? 6) Should you decide fast or slow on a hard-to-reverse choice? Answers: 1) Unfreeze, change, refreeze. 2) Establish a sense of urgency. 3) Competence, status, security, or control (any two). 4) Consult. 5) They show progress and build momentum and belief that the change can work. 6) Slow down for hard-to-reverse decisions."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: declaring victory after the first win, so the change never anchors and slides back. Mistake: using consensus for every decision, which stalls urgent calls, or deciding alone when you needed others'' knowledge and buy-in. Exit: (a) What are Lewin''s three steps? (b) Why do people resist change, and what is the general remedy? Answers: unfreeze, change, refreeze; they fear loss and uncertainty, so involve them early, be honest about trade-offs, and name what will and will not change."}]}'::jsonb, 'People do not resist change so much as they resist loss and uncertainty. Address what they fear losing, and most ''resistance'' softens.', 14),
  (13, 'Capstone: a leadership scenario and your self-development plan', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Pull the whole course together by diagnosing a realistic leadership scenario, then build a concrete plan to develop yourself. Warm-up: name the four parts of emotional intelligence and the two conflict types before you start."},{"heading":"The scenario","text":"You have just been promoted to lead a team of six that you were part of last week. The team is talented but tired: a big project slipped, morale is low, two members quietly disagree about the technical direction, and one strong performer has gone silent in meetings since the deadline was missed. Your manager wants a turnaround plan in two weeks. Work through it with the course tools. Where is the team on Tuckman''s stages, and what would move it forward? What would you do first to rebuild psychological safety? Is the technical disagreement task or relationship conflict, and which handling style fits? What might be behind the silent performer''s withdrawal, and how would you open that conversation? Which decisions here are decide-alone, consult, or consensus? Which motivators, in Herzberg''s sense, could you restore?"},{"heading":"A worked pass through the scenario","text":"Diagnosis: the missed deadline and low morale suggest the team has bounced back toward storming; your job is to help it re-norm. First move on safety: hold a blameless retro, share your own part in the slip first, and ask ''what made this hard?'' rather than ''who dropped it?'' The technical disagreement is task conflict, so keep it on the ideas — get both designs on a whiteboard against a shared goal (collaborating), and if time is short, you may have to decide and explain why. The silent performer likely fears blame or feels unheard; open a private one-on-one with facts and intent: ''I''ve noticed you''ve been quieter since the deadline (facts); I value your thinking and want to understand what''s going on (intent) — how are you finding things?'' Then listen. Decision modes: you consult the team on how to re-plan the project, but you decide alone and fast on the immediate client message. Motivators: hand back some autonomy on how the rework is done, mark a quick early win to rebuild belief, and reconnect the work to its purpose. None of this is a script — it is the toolkit applied."},{"heading":"Your turn: build your self-development plan","text":"Write a short plan using what you have learned. 1) Pick two leadership strengths and two growth areas — and note how you know (feedback is the best evidence). 2) Turn each growth area into one behavioral, observable goal for the next month. 3) For each goal, name the concrete practice and how you will measure it. 4) Name one person who will give you honest feedback, and when you will ask. Sample answers, as a model of the right shape: 1) Strengths: clear written communication, staying calm under pressure; growth areas: I interrupt in meetings, I jump to solutions before listening (two peers told me). 2) Goals: ''let others finish before I speak in every meeting this month''; ''ask at least one open question before advising in every one-on-one.'' 3) Practice: keep a tally of interruptions after each meeting; review one-on-one notes weekly for open questions. 4) Ask my peer Sam for candid feedback at the end of each week. Your plan should be this specific — vague goals do not change behavior."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: writing development goals as traits (''be more empathetic'') instead of observable behaviors you can practice and check. Mistake: making the turnaround about the plan and forgetting the people, when it is the people who will deliver it. Exit: (a) What makes a self-development goal actionable? (b) In the scenario, what is the first thing to rebuild, and how? Answers: it is behavioral, observable, and measurable within a set time; psychological safety, rebuilt through a blameless retro where you name your own part first and respond to problems with curiosity rather than blame."}]}'::jsonb, 'A development goal is only real when it is behavioral and observable — ''ask one open question before giving advice in every one-on-one this month'' beats ''be a better listener.''', 16),
  (14, 'Final quiz: comprehensive review', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Which pairing correctly matches the work to its focus?","options":["Management copes with change; leadership copes with complexity","Management copes with complexity; leadership copes with change","Both cope only with complexity","Both cope only with change"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Management plans and controls a known system; leadership sets direction and drives change."},{"prompt":"A leader who inspires with a compelling vision and gives individual attention is displaying which style?","options":["transactional","transformational","avoiding","accommodating"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Transformational leadership motivates through vision and individualized support."},{"prompt":"The foundation of emotional intelligence, on which the others rest, is…","options":["relationship management","self-awareness","persuasion","social awareness"],"answer":1,"explanation":"You cannot manage or use what you have not first noticed in yourself."},{"prompt":"On Herzberg''s theory, improving pay and working conditions will typically…","options":["create strong lasting motivation","remove dissatisfaction but not by itself motivate","reduce performance","do nothing at all"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Pay and conditions are hygiene factors; motivators like achievement drive engagement."},{"prompt":"The three elements of the autonomy-mastery-purpose frame are…","options":["pay, perks, promotion","autonomy, mastery, purpose","power, money, status","safety, esteem, belonging"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Self-determination research highlights control over work, getting better, and meaning."},{"prompt":"Tuckman''s five stages, in order, are…","options":["forming, norming, storming, performing, adjourning","storming, forming, norming, performing, adjourning","forming, storming, norming, performing, adjourning","norming, forming, storming, adjourning, performing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Forming, storming, norming, performing, then adjourning."},{"prompt":"The situation-behavior-impact structure is a tool for…","options":["running a budget","giving specific behavioral feedback","scheduling meetings","hiring"],"answer":1,"explanation":"It names the situation, the observable behavior, and its impact so feedback is actionable."},{"prompt":"In the Thomas-Kilmann model, a style that is high on both assertiveness and cooperativeness is…","options":["avoiding","competing","collaborating","accommodating"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Collaborating seeks a solution meeting both sides'' real interests."},{"prompt":"Moving from positions to interests in a conflict means…","options":["insisting harder on your demand","uncovering the underlying need behind each demand","splitting every difference","avoiding the topic"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Interests are the real needs behind stated positions and often overlap, opening solutions."},{"prompt":"When you delegate a task well, you transfer the work and the authority but keep…","options":["nothing","accountability for the outcome","all the credit","the right to redo it yourself"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Accountability stays with the delegator even as responsibility is handed over."},{"prompt":"The steps of the GROW coaching model are…","options":["Goal, Reality, Options, Will","Grow, Review, Own, Win","Goal, Roles, Outcome, Work","Gather, Rank, Order, Weigh"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Goal, Reality, Options, Will — questions that help someone find their own path forward."},{"prompt":"In Lewin''s change model, making the new way stick as the normal state is called…","options":["unfreeze","change","refreeze","storming"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Refreeze anchors the new behavior so the organization does not slide back."},{"prompt":"A production outage that needs an immediate call is best handled with which decision mode?","options":["build consensus","decide alone (you have the info and speed matters)","avoid deciding","wait for the annual review"],"answer":1,"explanation":"When speed matters and you have the information, decide alone; consult or build consensus when knowledge or buy-in is essential and time allows."},{"prompt":"Most resistance to change is best understood as resistance to…","options":["novelty for its own sake","loss and uncertainty","meetings","leadership itself"],"answer":1,"explanation":"People fear losing competence, status, security, or control; address that and resistance softens."}]}'::jsonb, null, 12)
) as v(pos, title, kind, body, notes, mins)
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.lessons l
  where l.course_id = 'bbbb0003-0000-4000-8000-000000000019' and l.position = v.pos
);
